{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Octane table, then shows how to layer on your first feature."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/octane-table","lang":"bash"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"your-first-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your First Table"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The component below is complete. Paste it into an Octane "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".tsrx"},{"type":"text","value":" file and you will see a working table. Octane components use a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@{ ... }"},{"type":"text","value":" component body, and keyed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@for"},{"type":"text","value":" blocks render dynamic lists."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. Give your data a stable reference (module scope, useState, a query cache, etc.)\nconst data: Array<Person> = [\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n]\n\n// 3. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 4. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n    cell: (info) => <i>{info.getValue<string>()}</i>,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\nexport function PersonTable() @{\n  // 5. Create the table instance\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n\n  // 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      @for (const headerGroup of table.getHeaderGroups(); key headerGroup.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const header of headerGroup.headers; key header.id) {\n            <th>\n              {header.isPlaceholder ? null : <table.FlexRender header={header} />}\n            </th>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      @for (const row of table.getRowModel().rows; key row.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const cell of row.getAllCells(); key cell.id) {\n            <td><table.FlexRender cell={cell} /></td>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A few things to note:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({})"},{"type":"text","value":" declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({...})"},{"type":"text","value":" call when you need them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" renders the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footer"},{"type":"text","value":" definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values or Octane components."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Octane compiles your application and the Table adapter's authored TypeScript and TSRX for the current target and mode."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the full "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-use-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic useTable example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a runnable version with more columns and a footer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a Feature: Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSortingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and the sorted row model in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", then wire the header click handler."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns, // built-in sort functions\n})\n\nexport function PersonTable() @{\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      @for (const headerGroup of table.getHeaderGroups(); key headerGroup.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const header of headerGroup.headers; key header.id) {\n            <th>\n              {header.isPlaceholder ? null : (\n                <div\n                  className={header.column.getCanSort() ? 'sortable-header' : ''}\n                  onClick={header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n                >\n                  <table.FlexRender header={header} />\n                  {header.column.getIsSorted() === 'asc'\n                    ? ' 🔼'\n                    : header.column.getIsSorted() === 'desc'\n                      ? ' 🔽'\n                      : null}\n                </div>\n              )}\n            </th>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </thead>\n    {/* tbody unchanged from above */}\n  </table>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model factory in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", then use the APIs those features add to the table, columns, and rows. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/sorting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to Go Next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table state."}]},{"type":"text","value":" In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" for starting values and call feature APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.nextPage()"},{"type":"text","value":". When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/table-state.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It is the foundational guide for everything else."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature guides."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each feature has its own guide, such as "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/column-filtering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Filtering"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/pagination.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/row-selection.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/column-visibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Visibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable tables."}]},{"type":"text","value":" When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/composable-tables.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable Tables Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Browse the runnable "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-use-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Octane examples"}]},{"type":"text","value":", from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"your-first-table","text":"Your First Table","level":2},{"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","text":"Add a Feature: Sorting","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to Go Next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Octane table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/octane-table\n```\n\n## Your First Table\n\nThe component below is complete. Paste it into an Octane `.tsrx` file and you will see a working table. Octane components use a `@{ ... }` component body, and keyed `@for` blocks render dynamic lists.\n\n```tsx\nimport { tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. Give your data a stable reference (module scope, useState, a query cache, etc.)\nconst data: Array<Person> = [\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n]\n\n// 3. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 4. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n    cell: (info) => <i>{info.getValue<string>()}</i>,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\nexport function PersonTable() @{\n  // 5. Create the table instance\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n\n  // 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      @for (const headerGroup of table.getHeaderGroups(); key headerGroup.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const header of headerGroup.headers; key header.id) {\n            <th>\n              {header.isPlaceholder ? null : <table.FlexRender header={header} />}\n            </th>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      @for (const row of table.getRowModel().rows; key row.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const cell of row.getAllCells(); key cell.id) {\n            <td><table.FlexRender cell={cell} /></td>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n}\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the `tableFeatures({...})` call when you need them.\n- `table.FlexRender` renders the `header`, `cell`, and `footer` definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values or Octane components.\n- Octane compiles your application and the Table adapter's authored TypeScript and TSRX for the current target and mode.\n\nSee the full [Basic useTable example](./examples/basic-use-table) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and the sorted row model in `tableFeatures`, then wire the header click handler.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns, // built-in sort functions\n})\n\nexport function PersonTable() @{\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      @for (const headerGroup of table.getHeaderGroups(); key headerGroup.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const header of headerGroup.headers; key header.id) {\n            <th>\n              {header.isPlaceholder ? null : (\n                <div\n                  className={header.column.getCanSort() ? 'sortable-header' : ''}\n                  onClick={header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n                >\n                  <table.FlexRender header={header} />\n                  {header.column.getIsSorted() === 'asc'\n                    ? ' 🔼'\n                    : header.column.getIsSorted() === 'desc'\n                      ? ' 🔽'\n                      : null}\n                </div>\n              )}\n            </th>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </thead>\n    {/* tbody unchanged from above */}\n  </table>\n}\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model factory in `tableFeatures`, then use the APIs those features add to the table, columns, and rows. See the [Sorting Guide](./guide/sorting.md) and the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md). It is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature guides.** Each feature has its own guide, such as [Column Filtering](./guide/column-filtering.md), [Pagination](./guide/pagination.md), [Row Selection](./guide/row-selection.md), and [Column Visibility](./guide/column-visibility.md).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })\n```\n\nSee the [Composable Tables Guide](./guide/composable-tables.md) for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Octane examples](./examples/basic-use-table), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Quick Start\n---\n\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Octane table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/octane-table\n```\n\n## Your First Table\n\nThe component below is complete. Paste it into an Octane `.tsrx` file and you will see a working table. Octane components use a `@{ ... }` component body, and keyed `@for` blocks render dynamic lists.\n\n```tsx\nimport { tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. Give your data a stable reference (module scope, useState, a query cache, etc.)\nconst data: Array<Person> = [\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n]\n\n// 3. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 4. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n    cell: (info) => <i>{info.getValue<string>()}</i>,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\nexport function PersonTable() @{\n  // 5. Create the table instance\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n\n  // 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      @for (const headerGroup of table.getHeaderGroups(); key headerGroup.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const header of headerGroup.headers; key header.id) {\n            <th>\n              {header.isPlaceholder ? null : <table.FlexRender header={header} />}\n            </th>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      @for (const row of table.getRowModel().rows; key row.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const cell of row.getAllCells(); key cell.id) {\n            <td><table.FlexRender cell={cell} /></td>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n}\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the `tableFeatures({...})` call when you need them.\n- `table.FlexRender` renders the `header`, `cell`, and `footer` definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values or Octane components.\n- Octane compiles your application and the Table adapter's authored TypeScript and TSRX for the current target and mode.\n\nSee the full [Basic useTable example](./examples/basic-use-table) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and the sorted row model in `tableFeatures`, then wire the header click handler.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns, // built-in sort functions\n})\n\nexport function PersonTable() @{\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  })\n\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      @for (const headerGroup of table.getHeaderGroups(); key headerGroup.id) {\n        <tr>\n          @for (const header of headerGroup.headers; key header.id) {\n            <th>\n              {header.isPlaceholder ? null : (\n                <div\n                  className={header.column.getCanSort() ? 'sortable-header' : ''}\n                  onClick={header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()}\n                >\n                  <table.FlexRender header={header} />\n                  {header.column.getIsSorted() === 'asc'\n                    ? ' 🔼'\n                    : header.column.getIsSorted() === 'desc'\n                      ? ' 🔽'\n                      : null}\n                </div>\n              )}\n            </th>\n          }\n        </tr>\n      }\n    </thead>\n    {/* tbody unchanged from above */}\n  </table>\n}\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model factory in `tableFeatures`, then use the APIs those features add to the table, columns, and rows. See the [Sorting Guide](./guide/sorting.md) and the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md). It is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature guides.** Each feature has its own guide, such as [Column Filtering](./guide/column-filtering.md), [Pagination](./guide/pagination.md), [Row Selection](./guide/row-selection.md), and [Column Visibility](./guide/column-visibility.md).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })\n```\n\nSee the [Composable Tables Guide](./guide/composable-tables.md) for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Octane examples](./examples/basic-use-table), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n"}
